(1) Name and address of the person to be notified of auction;
(2) Graphic scale, north point, and date;
(3) Location sketch map showing the location of the proposed auction to the surrounding area;
(4) Total acreage involved in the auction;
(5) Scale of not less than one inch equals 100 feet;
(6) Topography by contours at vertical intervals of not more than five feet with topographic survey based on a field survey or an aerial survey;
(7) Deed record names of all adjoining property owners;
(8) Locations of streams, lakes, swamps, wetlands, and the 100-year floodplain shall be shown;
(9) Location of buildings on the property to be auctioned;
(10) Location and right-of-way of streets, roads, railroads, and utilities on or adjacent to the property to be auctioned, specifying whether utility lines are in easements or right-of-way and showing location of towers;
(11) Size and location of existing sewers, water mains, drains, culverts, fire hydrants or other underground facilities within the tract or within the right-of-way of streets or roads adjoining the tract. Grades and inverts elevations of sewers shall be shown;
(12) Layout of all lots, including building setback lines, scaled dimensions on lots; utility easements with width and use;
(13) Certification by a registered engineer or surveyor attesting to the accuracy of the survey and plat;
(14) Certification by the health department that a proposed sewage system on each lot or tract would fully meet the requirements of the health department;
(15) Any protective covenants or deed restrictions for any lot or tract shall be placed directly on the plat. If there are no such restrictions, then a statement of such shall be placed on the plat;
(16) The following certifications must be obtained and recorded on the plat:
a. Engineer or surveyor.
